§ `VerifyCoveredAlternativesFixture`

-- An alternative the drafter wrote that the others already cover: every case
-- it lets through, the alternatives beside it let through too, so the rule
-- reads the same without it. This is the dual of a condition its siblings
-- already entail (verify-written-guards.l4), and it is reported the same way,
-- as a `vacuous-guard`, at the place it sits in the rule as written.

-- Reported.

-- (1) The first alternative is absorbed by the second.
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `absorbed` a b IF
    (a AND b) OR b

-- (2) The same, under a NOT. Deleting the alternative leaves what the NOT
--     applies to unchanged, so it leaves the rule unchanged too.
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `absorbed under a NOT` a b IF
    NOT ((a AND b) OR b)

-- (3) Inside a condition, with a third alternative beside the two.
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN, c IS A BOOLEAN, d IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `absorbed inside a condition` a b c d IF
    d AND (a OR c OR (a AND b))

-- Not reported.

-- (4) Each alternative lets through a case the other does not.
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN, c IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `two ways in` a b c IF
    (a AND b) OR (a AND c)

-- (5) The whole alternative is redundant here, and is reported as a condition
--     of the conjunction. Inside it, `b` is covered by `a` only because the
--     context already holds `a`, which makes every alternative covered; that
--     is the finding one level up, and it is not reported again.
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `covered by the context` a b IF
    a AND (b OR a)

-- (6) Two alternatives that between them let every case through: the
--     condition they make is true on its own and excludes nothing, which is a
--     different defect, and is not looked for (see the note on what a clean
--     run proves). Neither alternative is covered by the other.
GIVEN x IS A BOOLEAN, y IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a condition true on its own` x y IF
    x AND (y OR NOT y)

-- (7) Two alternatives that cover each other: each is reported, although
--     deleting either one alone leaves the rule as it was. The help and the
--     doc page say so.
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `covering each other` a b IF
    (a AND b) OR (b AND a)
